course: books
lesson: 35
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter III, part 3: The Wild Wood (the Rat finds the Mole, and the snow comes)
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Rat marches into the darkening wood. Wicked little faces pop out of holes — and vanish at the sight of him, his pistols and his big ugly stick; the whistling and pattering stop. For an hour or more he searches the whole wood, calling cheerfully, «Moly, Moly, Moly! Where are you? It's me—it's old Rat!»
explain: At last a feeble voice answers from an old beech tree: «Ratty! Is that really you?» The Mole is worn out and still shaking. The Rat explains: river-bankers never come here alone; you need to know the passwords, signs, sayings, plants and tricks — unless you're Badger or Otter. And Toad wouldn't come here alone for a hatful of gold!
explain: The Rat's laughter and his gleaming pistols cheer the Mole up. But he's too tired to walk, so they rest in the dry leaves; the Mole sleeps, and the Rat keeps watch with a pistol in his paw.
explain: When the Rat peeps out — «Snow is up ... or rather, down.» The scary wood has turned white and magical. But the snow hides every path, and the Rat doesn't know where they are.
explain: For an hour or two they stumble on, pretending every tree is an old friend, falling into holes, soaked and bruised. Hopelessly lost, they sit on a fallen log. The Rat has a plan: go down into a lumpy little valley ahead and find a dry cave or hole to rest in.
fact: «Here and there wicked little faces popped out of holes, but vanished immediately at sight of the valorous animal, his pistols, and the great ugly cudgel in his grasp» (valorous = brave).
fact: He searched «the whole ground, and all the time calling out cheerfully, "Moly, Moly, Moly! Where are you? It's me—it's old Rat!"»
fact: After «an hour or more» he heard «a little answering cry» from an old beech tree: «Ratty! Is that really you?» The Mole: «O Rat! I've been so frightened, you can't think!»
fact: The Rat: «You shouldn't really have gone and done it, Mole. I did my best to keep you from it. We river-bankers, we hardly ever come here by ourselves. If we have to come, we come in couples, at least».
fact: «there are a hundred things one has to know ... passwords, and signs, and sayings which have power and effect, and plants you carry in your pocket, and verses you repeat, and dodges and tricks you practise».
fact: «Of course if you were Badger or Otter, it would be quite another matter.»
fact: On Toad: «He wouldn't show his face here alone, not for a whole hatful of golden guineas, Toad wouldn't.» (a guinea = an old gold coin).
fact: «The Mole was greatly cheered by the sound of the Rat's careless laughter, as well as by the sight of his stick and his gleaming pistols».
fact: The Mole: «I'm simply dead beat and that's a solid fact» (dead beat = completely worn out). The Rat let him sleep, and «lay patiently waiting, with a pistol in his paw».
fact: «"What's up, Ratty?" — "Snow is up," replied the Rat briefly; "or rather, down. It's snowing hard."» — a little joke on «What's up?»
fact: The snow made the wood «a gleaming carpet of faery» (faery = fairyland) that «looked too delicate to be trodden upon by rough feet», and «a fine powder filled the air and caressed the cheek with a tingle».
fact: The Rat: «The worst of it is, I don't exactly know where we are. And now this snow makes everything look so very different.»
fact: They pretended «with invincible cheerfulness that they recognized an old friend in every fresh tree»; after an hour or two they were «dispirited, weary, and hopelessly at sea» (at sea = completely lost and confused).
fact: «There seemed to be no end to this wood, and no beginning, and no difference in it, and, worst of all, no way out.»
fact: The Rat's plan: a dell (small valley) «where the ground seems all hilly and humpy and hummocky» — look for «a cave or hole with a dry floor to it» to rest; «the snow may leave off, or something may turn up».
fact: Talk in this lesson: «knackered» (UK) = worn out; «no cap» = honestly; «wee» (Scotland) = small; «sound» (Liverpool) = fine; «lowkey» = quietly, a bit; «wicked» (UK) = brilliant.
exercise: What happens to the wicked faces when they see the Rat? | They vanish | They follow him all the way through the wood and whistle | They laugh at him | They attack him
exercise: Why do the faces vanish? | They see his pistols, his cudgel and his courage | They are all asleep by now, as it is getting late | They are scared of snow | They see Badger
exercise: What does the Rat call out as he searches? | «Moly, Moly, Moly! ... It's me—it's old Rat!» | «Badger! Open up!» | «Get out of this!» | «Poop-poop!»
exercise: How long does the Rat search before he hears an answer? | An hour or more | About five minutes | All night until dawn | Two whole days
exercise: Where does the Mole's voice come from? | An old beech tree with a hole in it | A rabbit-hole under a hedge at the edge of the wood | A ditch | A cave
exercise: What does the Mole cry when he sees the Rat? | «I've been so frightened, you can't think!» | «Go away, I'm perfectly all right here on my own, thank you very much!» | «I found Badger!» | «Hullo, Rat!»
exercise: How do river-bankers visit the Wild Wood, if they must? | In couples, at least | Alone, at midnight, with a lantern | By boat, along the river | Never at all, not even once
exercise: Which is NOT on the Rat's list of things you need to know? | The way to Toad Hall | Passwords you must whisper | Plants you carry in your pocket | Verses you repeat when in trouble
exercise: For whom would the Wild Wood be «quite another matter»? | Badger or Otter | Toad | The Mole | Rabbits
exercise: What wouldn't make Toad come to the Wild Wood alone? | A hatful of golden guineas | A brand-new motor-car with glittering plate-glass and rich leather seats | A free lunch | A map
exercise: What cheers the Mole up? | The Rat's laughter and his pistols | A hot drink of cocoa from the Rat's flask | Badger | A song
exercise: Why can't they leave at once? | The Mole is dead beat | The Rat has hurt his leg and can't walk at all | The faces block the way | It's raining far too hard
exercise: What does the Rat do while the Mole sleeps? | Waits with a pistol in his paw | Sleeps too, very soundly | Writes poetry | Goes for help
exercise: «"What's up, Ratty?" "Snow is up ... or rather, down."» What is the joke? | «What's up?» means «what's wrong», but snow falls down | Snow really does fall upwards in the Wild Wood, because the wood is magic | The Rat is upside down | A riddle
exercise: What does the snow turn the wood into? | A gleaming carpet of faery | A muddy swamp full of holes | A lake | A desert
exercise: What is the worst thing about the snow, says the Rat? | He doesn't know where they are | It is far too cold for the Mole to sleep, and his paws are freezing | The faces return | It's wet
exercise: How do they keep their spirits up while walking? | Pretending each tree is an old friend | Singing the Ducks' Ditty as loudly as they can, verse after verse | Telling jokes | Eating
exercise: «hopelessly at sea». What does it mean here? | Completely lost | On a boat | By the seaside | Very wet
exercise: Where do they sit to get their breath back? | On a fallen tree-trunk | In the beech tree again | On top of a low stone wall | On an old door-mat in the snow
exercise: Which is NOT one of their troubles in the snow? | The pistols are lost | Aching with tiredness and falling into holes | Bruised with tumbles | Wet through
exercise: What does the Rat suggest? | Look for a dry cave or hole in the dell ahead | Go back to the beech tree and wait for spring | Build a snowman | Climb a tree to look
exercise: «all hilly and humpy and hummocky». What is a hummock? | A small mound of earth | A kind of hammock | A badger's tail | A tree
test: Zoe, lowkey wicked (brilliant) chapter: «the Rat crept in quietly, scared stiff.» Put it right. | He went in boldly, calling cheerfully | Right: he tiptoed in shaking all over, too scared to call out | He drove a car in | He didn't go
test: Why does the Rat call out «cheerfully», not fearfully? | To sound brave and comfort the Mole | Because he thinks the wood is lovely and safe | He's singing | He's joking
test: The Rat says «You shouldn't really have gone and done it». Is he angry? | No — he's gentle and kind about it | Yes, he shouts at the Mole and refuses to take him home | Yes, very | He doesn't speak
test: Harry, knackered (worn out) after footy: «the Mole was dead beat too, so they set off home at once.» Put it right. | They rested first and the Mole slept | Right: they ran all the way home before the snow | They stayed till spring | The Rat slept
test: Why would Toad not come here alone «for a whole hatful of golden guineas»? | He's a coward, for all his boasting | He's far too rich to need any more guineas | He hates hats | He's in Town
test: The Mole asked «Surely the brave Mr. Toad wouldn't mind coming here?» Why does the Rat laugh? | Toad isn't brave — he only talks big | Toad is braver than anyone, and the Rat finds that funny | Toad is lost | He's tired
test: What would have happened if the snow had not fallen? | They could see their way home | The faces would have stayed away for ever | They'd have frozen | Nothing changes
test: The snow looks «too delicate to be trodden upon». Is the snow good news or bad news for them? | Both: beautiful, but it hides the way | Only good: it shows them the way home, like a white road | Only bad | Neither
test: Looking back: what was the Mole's state at the end of part 2? | Hiding in a beech tree, trembling, knowing the Terror | Asleep at home in an armchair by the fire | With Badger | With the rabbits
test: Looking back: in part 1 the Rat said of the Wild Wood «we won't go there just now». What does he now add? | River-bankers come in couples, at least | They never go there, ever, under any circumstances | They go every week | Only Toad goes
test: Looking back: in chapter I the Rat said: «there's Badger, of course ... Nobody interferes with him». How does that fit «if you were Badger, it would be quite another matter»? | Badger is strong, so the Wood is safe for him | Badger is great friends with the weasels and stoats and goes to all their parties | Badger flies | Badger moved
test: Looking back: in chapter II, what did the Mole whisper to the homesick Rat on the common? | He'd go home with him early | «Let's buy a car» | «I love the road» | «Toad's a fool»
test: Looking back: in chapter II, the Rat stayed with Toad because «it wouldn't be safe for him to be left to himself». Who is the Rat looking after now? | The Mole | Toad of Toad Hall | Old Mr. Badger | The Otter
test: Looking back: the Mole went into the wood partly to meet whom? | Mr. Badger | The kind Otter | Toad of Toad Hall | The weasels
test: ★★★ «There seemed to be no end to this wood, and no beginning, and no difference in it». What is the trap — what is the wood really like? | It has an end; it only feels endless because they are lost | It truly goes on for ever, round the world | It has no trees | It is tiny
test: ★★★ Put in order: (a) snow begins; (b) the Rat finds the Mole; (c) the Mole sleeps; (d) they sit on a fallen tree-trunk. | b, c, a, d | a, b, c, d | b, a, c, d | c, b, a, d
test: ★★★ The Rat lists «plants you carry in your pocket» and other things you must know. Did the Mole have any of them? | No — he knew none of them | Yes: Toad had taught him all the passwords and given him a lucky plant | Yes, a pistol | Yes, a map
exam: Identify the effect produced upon the creatures of the Wild Wood by the appearance of the Rat. | They vanished immediately | They pursued him through the trees | They whistled in alarm and attacked | They welcomed him in a friendly way
exam: Ascertain the practice of river-bankers, as stated by the Rat, in respect of visits to the Wild Wood. | They come in couples at least, if they must come | They come alone at night | They never return | They come by boat
exam: Select the response which most accurately characterises the Rat's estimate of Toad's courage. | Toad would not come alone for a hatful of guineas | That Toad visits the Wild Wood daily and unaccompanied, being the bravest of all animals | Toad is fearless | Toad lives there
exam: It is given that snow had fallen. Identify the principal difficulty which it occasioned. | It concealed every landmark, so that the Rat knew not where they were | It extinguished the Rat's lantern | It froze the river | It awakened the Badger
